# Cross-platform compatibility

Every surface must run on iOS, Android, and Web. No platform is optional; "works on my
simulator" is not a definition of done.

## Runtime baseline

- React 19+, React Native 0.80+ with the **New Architecture** enabled, Hermes engine.
- `react-native-web` for the web target; TypeScript in `strict` mode.
- No global error boundary as a safety net for bad data — render paths must not throw;
  rules modules return typed fallbacks for malformed input.

## Verification surfaces

Before a change is complete, verify (or explicitly reason about) all of:

| Surface | How |
|---|---|
| iOS | Run on simulator/device with the target environment |
| Android | Run on emulator/device with the target environment |
| Web | Run the web bundle via `react-native-web` |
| Storybook | Stories render on native and web Storybook without console errors |

## Sanctioned platform divergence (only these)

- **Value-level divergence:** `Platform.select` / `Platform.OS` at the **smallest possible
  scope** (a single style value or prop) — never wrapping whole components.
- **Structural divergence:** a sibling `.web.tsx` / `.web.ts` file with the **same public
  API** as the native file. Shared logic stays in a platform-neutral `*Rules.ts`.
  Split into `.web.tsx` when more than 2–3 `Platform.OS === 'web'` branches accumulate.
- **Keyboard handling:** owned by the design-system keyboard primitives — no screen-level
  keyboard hacks.
- **Safe area:** owned by the page/modal layout primitives — screens do not call
  `useSafeAreaInsets` when already inside those layouts.

## Forbidden

- `process.platform` — use `Platform.OS`.
- DOM APIs (`document`, `window.*`) without a web guard **and** a non-web fallback.
- Dependencies without web support, unless isolated behind an adapter module with a
  web-safe implementation.
- Duplicating shared logic in platform files — extract to a neutral `*Rules.ts`.
